{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/873405-st-question-how-do-people-deal-with-skills-and-stats","id":873405,"title":"ST Question: How do people deal with skills and stats?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-04-24T17:33:19-07:00","id":873405,"title":"ST Question: How do people deal with skills and stats?","content":"I've been running a Werewolf game for about two &quot;seasons&quot; and I'm very easy going when it comes to skills and stat ranks. I have multiple people with fours in multiple stats and two with fives. A lot of these were started at a high level during character creation with bonus points.<br /> <br /> I'm curious how others feel in regards to skill ranks. Do people let their players start with Melee 5, and let them rank up easily? Or are people much more harsh regarding stats and skills?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7550-vulcan7200","name":"Vulcan7200","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-24T18:50:33-07:00","id":873442,"title":"","content":"For starting characters I have all skills kept to 3 with the exception that they can take one ability from skills, Talents, and knowledge and have it at 4. The backstory needs to explain why the character is so skilled in those traits.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1250-shadowlost","name":"Shadowlost","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"155"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-24T23:19:19-07:00","id":873555,"title":"","content":"At creation, I'm generally fairly lax. As far as I'm concerned, if it makes sense and fits the rules? Go for it.<br /> <br /> During play, it depends a lot on how long we're expecting the characters to be played consistently. The longer the campaign, the more strict I tend to get about advancement. This usually isn't really a problem. People understand that if we're only going to be playing for a few sessions, there's no reason to be picky. If we're going to be playing for two years, it helps the group to have stronger structure to keep the game sensible over a longer period of time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-25T01:45:48-07:00","id":873604,"title":"","content":"I make a story out of all of it. Having a Skill or an Attribute at 5 (or 1!) is something character defining. It makes you the greatest among the greats and leaves a mark on anyone who might know you. So if you want it, then make it matter, or you obviously don't care about having it <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> My games are usually measured in years, so it pays to make sure that people are consistant and actually keeping to character advancement as a part of of the game itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-25T06:07:08-07:00","id":873698,"title":"","content":"Isnt this more a general question?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-25T19:45:12-07:00","id":874206,"title":"","content":"I make it clear during character creation what the ranks actually mean. 4 is outstanding and 5 is world class. I also make it known that achieving those levels requires the character to devote a lot of time in game to accomplish. Their actions in game justify the expenditure of XP. I simply don't allow it just because they have the necessary XP.<br /> <br /> During character creation I know I'll be seeing one 4 in Attributes. I also know I might see one or two Abilities at 4 as well. Some players have their heart set at having an attribute or ability at 5, and if so, I let them. But it is clear that is exceptional and is a noteworthy trait. I make it known their background really needs to incorporate that.<br /> <br /> I also try my best to run games where ranks of 3 can get lots of things done. I try to minimize rolling dice all the time as that encourages people to increase their ranks just so they can accomplish mundane stuff or be halfway competent.<br /> <br /> There is one PC with a strength of 5, but his dexterity is only 2 so it's not like he's unstoppable in combat. And most of his downtime (when people are doing other stuff with their characters) is basically spent just maintaining that level of physical strength. He is in constant training. The first thing is the role play/concept, and the mechanics simply support it.<br /> <br /> The people I game with are very good so I trust them not to get ridiculous. I've found it's not so much what you allow players to do, as it is to have good players who want the same thing out of the game as you do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-26T12:28:03-07:00","id":874782,"title":"","content":"Generally speaking, I allow just about anything as long as there's a narrative explanation to go with it. With that in mind, obviously people will justify literally anything if they're given free reign to fabricate whatever backstory they want (e.g., &quot;I'm an Olympic power lifter and kendo champion who was also a Navy SEAL&quot;).<br /> <br /> If the explanation is tonally inconsistent with the rest of the game, is simply unjustifiable within the canon (assuming you're using the canon), or the character build would logically have other skills and abilities that the person doesn't have the points to make (i.e., in my example above, a Navy SEAL would have such a wide variety of abilities at at least 3 that it's unlikely anyone is going to make a starting character that accurate reflects the abilities an actual SEAL would have), then I veto. If it passes narrative muster but was obviously min-maxed, I warn that I'm going to make their &quot;mins&quot; of overwhelming importance--I've rarely had a player just take their chances after that warning.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-26T16:39:25-07:00","id":874988,"title":"","content":"Since I started GMing a while back, I let my players allow to have one attribute and skill/talent/knowledge on 5, so that was a defining piece of their character and a reason they were embraced . Well since this is a V20 chronicle, it might be a little different and I am also pretty laid back as long as it makes some kind of sense. <br /> For werewolf I would be a little harsher in the beginning, don't ask me why just a gut feeling.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5970-meldok","name":"Meldok","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-26T22:06:42-07:00","id":875282,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowlost</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/873405-st-question-how-do-people-deal-with-skills-and-stats?p=873442#post873442\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For starting characters I have all skills kept to 3 with the exception that they can take one ability from skills, Talents, and knowledge and have it at 4. The backstory needs to explain why the character is so skilled in those traits.</div> </div> </div> </div>I often do similar when starting out new pcs in groups. I let them know, they can't have more than ability in each group at 4 (or more) without getting it worked out with me first, such as cause they have totem points assigned etc).. <br /> <br /> And as for raising them in game, i often default to you need to find someone to teach you to level a skill up.. so they need that level (or higher) to teach!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-27T11:54:59-07:00","id":875815,"title":"","content":"I'd be careful about advancing skills to 5 or even quickly to 4 as the chronicle progresses, as well. Experience points can't really substitute for years and years of training. Making the game play well with three dots in abilities is the key, I'd agree.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2214-traveller","name":"Traveller","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2214_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T00:21:47-07:00","id":876325,"title":"","content":"I think it is a problem to attribute concrete equivalents of a level in a given skill to a system that is supposed to be abstract. Having a dot or two or five in a given skill is first and foremost a die in any dicepool with that skill. And, unless you use the instruction skill and it rules, there is hardly any guideline on how long it takes to raise any stat (save for gifts, there is a rule of thumb for those). Rites on the other hand might call for weeks of teaching/learning, but no experience is required what so ever. Same goes for building instructions for talens, if you don't handwave them entirely, or require skills for them like Craft (basketweaving) and Craft (arrowmaking) and so on.<br /> <br /> Also, while it seems common knowledge that you need to train to maintain peek physical power, there are no rules that any attribute or skill a character has might decrease if not used - save for backgrounds and age depending attribute adjustments and Battle Scars. And thing is, we don't really know how fast such things happen. Sure, medicine scientists run tests like how heavy can weights be an astronaut can lift before and after his space voyage and stuff like that - but unless one is into sports medicine one can't really translate such effects into a five dot spread.<br /> <br /> For our LARP I established this rule: All regular, open-for-all games are 'increase relevant', and marked as such in our character database. To raise a stat a number of such games must have passed since the last raise (character start is considered a raise for all stats). I doesn't matter if you participated in the game (we don't blame you if you have to work and can't play), but of course you need the XP to raise a stat, too.<br /> <br /> So, for attributes so many games need to have passed since the last raise, as the level of the attribute. So, raise from 1 to 2 after one game is possible, raise from 2 to 3 is possible after two additional games, and so on.<br /> Skills from 0 to 1 or 1 to 2 is possible after one game each, 2 to 3 and 3 to 4 requires two games each, and 4 to 5 requires three games.<br /> Totem is considered an attribute for the games required.<br /> Willpower, Rage and Gnosis require one game to have passed for each raise up to 6 and two for each raise beyond that.<br /> <br /> With this method we prohibit that a character starts game with one low stat and raises it within two games to a high stat, even if the character has gotten the appropriate XP.<br /> <br /> Usually I would, in a table top game, allow for a player to raise every stat they feel suitable. But I consider the background of the character when I handwave rolls. Meaning if a theurge who recently raised medicine to 5 tries a difficult operation I let him roll. If a character/NPC who's backstory is that he is a trauma surgeon tries the same, I might handwave it, even if the skill rating is only 4.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T01:06:05-07:00","id":876341,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/873405-st-question-how-do-people-deal-with-skills-and-stats?p=876325#post876325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it is a problem to attribute concrete equivalents of a level in a given skill to a system that is supposed to be abstract. Having a dot or two or five in a given skill is first and foremost a die in any dicepool with that skill. And, unless you use the instruction skill and it rules, there is hardly any guideline on how long it takes to raise any stat (save for gifts, there is a rule of thumb for those). Rites on the other hand might call for weeks of teaching/learning, but no experience is required what so ever. Same goes for building instructions for talens, if you don't handwave them entirely, or require skills for them like Craft (basketweaving) and Craft (arrowmaking) and so on.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point. TO most (especially in skill point games) having X number of dots is just seen as 'extra dice' but no real concern is given to what that 'dot represents'.. Though some do... Such as in Starwars D6 (the old West end games version) having 4d in a skill is 'professional level' while being at say 8d is 'considered the best on a planet.. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/873405-st-question-how-do-people-deal-with-skills-and-stats?p=876325#post876325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, while it seems common knowledge that you need to train to maintain peek physical power, there are no rules that any attribute or skill a character has might decrease if not used - save for backgrounds and age depending attribute adjustments and Battle Scars. And thing is, we don't really know how fast such things happen. Sure, medicine scientists run tests like how heavy can weights be an astronaut can lift before and after his space voyage and stuff like that - but unless one is into sports medicine one can't really translate such effects into a five dot spread.</div> </div> </div> </div>That to me is one of the bigger downfalls for most Every game system out there. No rules for Losing something you don't continually use/train on.. Heck many don't even have rules for aging and what it does to the body, unlike say ADND which at least has concrete rules in what Str/Dex/Con you lose and what Int/Wis you gain..<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T01:33:23-07:00","id":876357,"title":"","content":"Werewolf had ageing rules too, in the Player's Guide 2nd Edition (and maybe reprinted in PGttG).<br /> <br /> RPGs aren't real life simulators, though. One could devise more or less complicated systems to raise and lower stats, but it would involve a lot of bookkeeping and certainly distract from the actual story that is played.<br /> <br /> I usually don't see the problem in keeping stats and real life equivalents separated. Trying to find exact representations is rarely satisfactory.<br /> <br /> There are RPGs that allow for a set of traits to be set to a certain level and the ST and player can decide if a given narrow task is within the scope of the trait set or not. Like being rocket scientist 4 or ER doctor 2 or White Claw Ninja 5.<br /> <br /> I usually prefer systems that don't have abstract skills at all. RuneQuest for example has traits in %-values. It is very easy to grasp how good someone is in a given task. If a task is more difficult than average, there is a penalty in % and likewise easy tasks have bonuses in % and it all in all is very intuitive and one can really easily transfer the likelihood of succeeding in a task of a given difficulty to a real life equivalent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T14:40:44-07:00","id":876991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/873405-st-question-how-do-people-deal-with-skills-and-stats?p=876357#post876357\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Werewolf had ageing rules too, in the Player's Guide 2nd Edition (and maybe reprinted in PGttG).<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm.. I might have to see if i can find them, cause at this time i don't actually remember reading any.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T23:39:20-07:00","id":877396,"title":"","content":"From the top of my head it's something akin to making Gnosis rolls once per year starting from a certain age. Difficulty is modified by previous rolls and Battle Scars. If not successful the character looses some trait (chosen by player and ST, iirc) and botch means the character will die of old age next winter.<br /> But I might be wrong. It is in the Appendix of one of the Player's Guides, with other nice treats like Abominations and such...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}